// The revl stdlib authorization module — roadmap item 457 Slice 2
// (docs/design/457-endpoint-one-definition.md, "Authorization: explicit, and
// not derivable"; Cordis alignment docs/design/529-sso-auth-alignment.md).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the problem
// An endpoint (a service operation with a `route` clause, Slice 1) makes an
// operation reachable over HTTP. The requirement the design is really after is
// that GENERATED metadata must never grant access, and that REMOVING the
// explicit authorization step denies the request — provably, not by convention.
// Neither the derived router, the OpenAPI document nor the TypeScript client
// carries anything that could stand in for an authorization decision (Slice 1
// pins that: the clause has no auth vocabulary and the export derives no
// `security`). What was missing was the value that IS the right to reach user
// data, and a way to make skipping the check impossible rather than merely
// unwise.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the fix
// Three declarations, and one checker invariant that ties them together.
//
//   * `Bearer` — the credential AS PRESENTED. A record carrying an optional
//     token, bound from the `Authorization` header by the Slice-1 route bind
//     table (the `x-revl-auth: bearer` marker). It is an untrusted CLAIM: it
//     decides nothing on its own.
//   * `Auth.validate` — the ONE producer of a `Principal`. It turns a `Bearer`
//     into `Result[Principal, ApiError]`: `Ok(who)` when the credential checks
//     out, `Err(unauthorized(...))` (a `401`) when it is missing or invalid.
//   * `Principal` — the opaque authorization principal. It has NO revl
//     declaration and NO constructor: no record literal, no variant case, no
//     `type Principal = ...` (the checker RESERVES the name and refuses such a
//     declaration). The only expression in the language whose type is
//     `Principal` is a call to `Auth.validate`.
//
// A user-scoped store or handler operation takes a `Principal`:
//
//     service NoteStore {
//       fn get(who: Principal, id: Str) -> Result[Note, ApiError]
//     }
//
// so the only path to a note runs through a value only `Auth.validate` mints.
// A handler that DROPS the validate step has no `Principal` in scope and cannot
// call `store.get`; the compiler refuses it AT ADMISSION, naming `Principal` and
// its sole producer (`typecheck.PRINCIPAL_PRODUCER_HINT`). That is the
// stronger-than-runtime guarantee: the mutant does not fail closed at runtime,
// it fails to type-check. The runtime twin still holds where the step is
// present — a missing/invalid credential is the `Err` from `validate`, rendered
// `401` by the Slice-1 return rules.
//
// The correct shape:
//
//     component NotesHttp requires auth: Auth, store: NoteStore
//                         provides notes_api: NotesApi {
//       provide notes_api {
//         fn get_note(bearer, id) {
//           return match auth.validate(bearer) {   // the explicit step
//             Err(e) => Err(e),                       // 401, from the auth service
//             Ok(who) => store.get(who, id),          // `who` is the only key
//           }
//         }
//       }
//     }
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- Cordis anchor
// On the ts tier the provider binds `Auth.validate` to Cordis SSO's
// `ctx.sso.validateSession` through a `host @auth` row over a shipped shim
// (docs/design/529-sso-auth-alignment.md). That host binding — the `host` row,
// `stdlib/server.rvl`, and the `@cordisjs/plugin-sso` shim — is Slice 3. The
// `Principal`/`Auth.validate` shape and the admission refusal are
// tier-independent and land here.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the validator
// `Auth.validate` is only as sound as `host_validate`, the body that mints the
// `Principal` (issue #1554, design docs/design/569-serve-http-caller-identity.md:
// `revl serve --http` authenticates nobody, so this body IS the authentication).
//
// Format: a signed JWT (RFC 7519) in JWS compact form (RFC 7515) with the
// HS256 algorithm (HMAC-SHA256, RFC 7518 section 3.2). Why this one:
//   * it is the token every identity provider and every JWT library can mint,
//     so an app does not have to invent a session format to use this module;
//   * HMAC-SHA256 verifies with the Python standard library alone (`hmac`,
//     `hashlib`), so revl keeps zero required runtime dependencies. An
//     asymmetric algorithm (EdDSA, ES256) would need `cryptography` on py;
//   * the claims the issue asks for are standard members: `exp` (expiry),
//     `aud` (audience), `iss` (issuer), and `sub` becomes the subject.
// Only `alg: "HS256"` is accepted. `none`, every other algorithm, a `crit`
// header, a `typ` other than `JWT`, duplicate JSON members, non-canonical
// base64url and tokens over 8 KiB are refused. The signature is checked in
// constant time BEFORE the claims are parsed. `exp`, `iss`, `aud` and `sub` are
// required; `nbf` is honoured when present.
//
// Configuration comes from the process environment only, never from the IR, a
// component config table (those are recorded with the composition) or a
// request:
//   REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET       the shared key, at least 32 bytes (RFC 7518)
//   REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET_FILE  or a file holding it (one trailing newline is
//                                dropped); set exactly one of the two
//   REVL_AUTH_ISSUER             the exact `iss` a token must carry
//   REVL_AUTH_AUDIENCE           the audience a token's `aud` must contain
//   REVL_AUTH_LEEWAY             optional clock-skew allowance in whole
//                                seconds, 0 to 300 (default 0)
// No refusal, log line or trace carries the key: it is read inside this body
// and never becomes a revl value (tests/test_auth_validator_1554.py pins it).
//
// The old any-token stub (a non-blank token is that subject) survives ONLY
// behind the explicit dev flag REVL_AUTH_INSECURE_DEV_STUB=1. With neither a
// validator configured nor the flag, every call is refused with a `503`
// `auth_not_configured` naming the variables to set: an unconfigured deployment
// never accepts silently. Setting the flag next to a configured validator is
// refused too, so a stale dev flag cannot shadow production keys. And
// `revl serve --http` refuses to START on a non-loopback address while the flag
// is set (design 569 question 5), so the stub never listens on the network.
//
// Tiers (issue #1554):
//   py            the validator above; the reference tier.
//   ts, rs, go,   no body: emitting a composition that composes this module is
//   java          refused at compile time naming `host_validate` and the
//                 backend. The ts binding is Cordis SSO (Slice 3, design 529).
//   wasm          refused at compile time: `Principal` is not lowerable there.
//
// Compose this file as a ROOT module of the composition (components are never
// `use`-imported): `compile_files(["your_app.rvl", "stdlib/auth.rvl"])`, and
// name the row `host @auth provides auth: Auth` once Slice 3 lands the shim.
//
// Import the surface with a named `use`:
//
//     use "stdlib/auth.rvl" { Auth, Bearer }
//
// `Principal` is NOT imported: it has no declaration to import — it is only ever
// the result of `Auth.validate` and a parameter type on the services that
// consume it.

use "stdlib/http.rvl" { ApiError }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- credential

// The bearer credential as presented on the wire — an UNTRUSTED claim. `token`
// is `Opt[Str]` because a request may carry no `Authorization` header at all;
// `None` is the honest "no credential", never a sentinel `""`. The Slice-1 route
// bind table binds a `Bearer` parameter from the single `Authorization` header
// (`docs/design/457-endpoint-one-definition.md`, "Binding rules"). A `Bearer`
// decides nothing: it is the INPUT to `Auth.validate`, and holding one grants no
// access until it is validated into a `Principal`.
pub type Bearer = { token: Opt[Str] }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the service

// The authorization service: the sole producer of a `Principal`. `validate`
// turns an untrusted `Bearer` into `Result[Principal, ApiError]` — `Ok(who)`
// when the credential checks out, `Err(unauthorized(...))` (a `401`) otherwise.
// It is a plain `fn` (non-emission): validating a credential reaches host auth
// state to READ it, it does not alter the running world, so a consumer sees from
// the declaration that calling it cannot emit. `Principal` is opaque and
// undeclared — no revl body can construct one, so this operation is the only
// door to it (checker-enforced: `typecheck.PRINCIPAL`).
service Auth {
  fn validate(b: Bearer) -> Result[Principal, ApiError]
}

// The single host body behind the crossing. On py it is the HS256 JWT validator
// described in the header; on ts it becomes the `host @auth` row over Cordis
// SSO's `ctx.sso.validateSession` (Slice 3). It is `pure` for the same reason
// `stdlib/fs.rvl`'s read ops are: it READS external state (its key, the clock)
// and performs no accounted mutation, so it carries no emission on the G8 audit
// surface. Because it reads the clock, its answer for one token changes when
// the token expires, so it must never be memoized. Its RESULT type is the
// opaque `Principal`, which no caller can otherwise obtain.
extern pure fn host_validate(b: Bearer) -> Result[Principal, ApiError] = @py {
    # A record arrives as a dict and `Opt[Str]` as the bare value or None.
    import base64, hashlib, hmac, json, math, os, time

    B64URL = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-_"
    MAX_TOKEN = 8192
    MIN_KEY = 32
    MAX_LEEWAY = 300
    DEV_FLAG = "REVL_AUTH_INSECURE_DEV_STUB"
    CONFIGURE = ("set REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET (or REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET_FILE), "
                 "REVL_AUTH_ISSUER and REVL_AUTH_AUDIENCE")

    def refuse(status, code, message):
        return Err({"status": status, "code": code, "message": message})

    def unconfigured(reason):
        return refuse(503, "auth_not_configured", "stdlib/auth: " + reason)

    def unauthorized(reason):
        return refuse(401, "unauthorized", "invalid credential: " + reason)

    def read_key():
        # (key bytes or None, problem or None). The key never enters a message.
        inline = os.environ.get("REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET", "")
        path = os.environ.get("REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET_FILE", "")
        if inline and path:
            return None, ("set only one of REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET and "
                          "REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET_FILE")
        if path:
            try:
                with open(path, "rb") as fh:
                    key = fh.read()
            except OSError:
                return None, "REVL_AUTH_HS256_SECRET_FILE cannot be read"
            if key.endswith(b"\n"):
                key = key[:-2] if key.endswith(b"\r\n") else key[:-1]
        elif inline:
            key = inline.encode("utf-8")
        else:
            return None, None
        if len(key) < MIN_KEY:
            return None, ("the HS256 key is shorter than 32 bytes "
                          "(RFC 7518 section 3.2)")
        return key, None

    def read_leeway():
        raw = os.environ.get("REVL_AUTH_LEEWAY", "").strip()
        if not raw:
            return 0, None
        if not (raw.isascii() and raw.isdigit()) or int(raw) > MAX_LEEWAY:
            return None, "REVL_AUTH_LEEWAY must be whole seconds from 0 to 300"
        return int(raw), None

    def settings():
        # ("stub" | a config dict | None, problem or None)
        dev = os.environ.get(DEV_FLAG, "")
        if dev not in ("", "1"):
            return None, DEV_FLAG + " must be exactly 1 or unset"
        key, problem = read_key()
        if problem:
            return None, problem
        if key is None:
            if dev == "1":
                return "stub", None
            return None, ("no token validator is configured: " + CONFIGURE +
                          "; the any-token test stub runs only with " +
                          DEV_FLAG + "=1")
        if dev:
            return None, (DEV_FLAG + " is set next to a configured validator; "
                          "unset one of them")
        issuer = os.environ.get("REVL_AUTH_ISSUER", "")
        audience = os.environ.get("REVL_AUTH_AUDIENCE", "")
        if not issuer or not audience:
            return None, "an HS256 key is set but " + CONFIGURE + " are all required"
        leeway, problem = read_leeway()
        if problem:
            return None, problem
        return {"key": key, "iss": issuer, "aud": audience,
                "leeway": leeway}, None

    def b64url(segment):
        # strict unpadded base64url; a non-canonical spelling is refused so one
        # signature has exactly one encoding
        if not segment or len(segment) % 4 == 1:
            raise ValueError("bad base64url")
        if any(c not in B64URL for c in segment):
            raise ValueError("bad base64url")
        raw = base64.urlsafe_b64decode(segment + "=" * (-len(segment) % 4))
        if base64.urlsafe_b64encode(raw).rstrip(b"=").decode("ascii") != segment:
            raise ValueError("non-canonical base64url")
        return raw

    def no_duplicates(pairs):
        obj = dict(pairs)
        if len(obj) != len(pairs):
            raise ValueError("duplicate member")
        return obj

    def no_constant(name):
        raise ValueError("non-finite number")

    def json_object(raw):
        value = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8"), object_pairs_hook=no_duplicates,
                           parse_constant=no_constant)
        if not isinstance(value, dict):
            raise ValueError("not an object")
        return value

    def header_problem(header):
        if header.get("alg") != "HS256":
            return "unsupported or missing alg (only HS256 is accepted)"
        if "crit" in header:
            return "unsupported crit header"
        if header.get("typ", "JWT") != "JWT":
            return "unsupported typ"
        return None

    def verified_claims(token, key):
        # (claims or None, reason or None); the signature is checked first
        if len(token) > MAX_TOKEN:
            return None, "token too large"
        parts = token.split(".")
        if len(parts) != 3:
            return None, "not a signed JWT"
        head, body, sig = parts
        try:
            header = json_object(b64url(head))
        except (ValueError, RecursionError):
            return None, "malformed header"
        reason = header_problem(header)
        if reason:
            return None, reason
        try:
            payload = b64url(body)
            signature = b64url(sig)
        except ValueError:
            return None, "malformed token"
        # both segments are checked base64url above, so they are ASCII
        signed = (head + "." + body).encode("ascii")
        expected = hmac.new(key, signed, hashlib.sha256).digest()
        if not hmac.compare_digest(expected, signature):
            return None, "signature does not verify"
        try:
            return json_object(payload), None
        except (ValueError, RecursionError):
            return None, "malformed claims"

    def is_number(v):
        if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, (int, float)):
            return False
        return isinstance(v, int) or math.isfinite(v)

    def time_problem(claims, leeway, now):
        exp = claims.get("exp")
        if not is_number(exp):
            return "token has no numeric exp"
        if now >= exp + leeway:
            return "token expired"
        nbf = claims.get("nbf")
        if nbf is not None and (not is_number(nbf) or now < nbf - leeway):
            return "token not yet valid"
        return None

    def audiences(aud):
        if isinstance(aud, str):
            return [aud]
        if isinstance(aud, list) and all(isinstance(a, str) for a in aud):
            return aud
        return []

    def claims_problem(claims, cfg, now):
        reason = time_problem(claims, cfg["leeway"], now)
        if reason:
            return reason
        if claims.get("iss") != cfg["iss"]:
            return "wrong issuer"
        if cfg["aud"] not in audiences(claims.get("aud")):
            return "wrong audience"
        sub = claims.get("sub")
        if not isinstance(sub, str) or not sub.strip():
            return "token has no subject"
        return None

    cfg, problem = settings()
    if problem:
        return unconfigured(problem)
    tok = b.get("token") if isinstance(b, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(tok, str) or not tok.strip():
        return refuse(401, "unauthorized", "missing credential")
    if cfg == "stub":
        # REVL_AUTH_INSECURE_DEV_STUB=1 only: a non-blank token is that subject
        return Ok({"subject": tok})
    claims, reason = verified_claims(tok, cfg["key"])
    if reason is None:
        reason = claims_problem(claims, cfg, time.time())
    if reason:
        return unauthorized(reason)
    return Ok({"subject": claims["sub"]})
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- provider

// The py-tier provider. Compose it as a root module alongside the app; on the ts
// tier a `host @auth` row supersedes it with the Cordis SSO binding (Slice 3).
component AuthProvider provides auth: Auth {
  provide auth {
    fn validate(b) = host_validate(b)
  }
}
